The '''X-League''' ({{nihongo|Xリーグ|Ekkusu Rīgu}}), officially the '''X-League Professional Lurandian Football''' ({{nihongo|Xリーグプロ鎧球|Ekkusu Rīgu Puro Gaikyū}}), is the highest level of professional [[Lurandian Football]] in [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 1985, the league currently comprises twelve corporate-backed teams organized into two divisions. The X-League is the second most-watched professional sports league in Okaiken, trailing only [[Okaiken Rugby League One]].

The league's season runs from September through January, with the regular season determining playoff seeding and the postseason culminating in the [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game. The X-League is known for its intense corporate-backed rivalries, deeply loyal fan bases, and a style of play that emphasizes aggressive passing, punishing special teams, and high-impact collisions consistent with the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} cultural movement.

The [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] are the league's most successful franchise, having won nine Rice Bowl titles. The current dominant storyline is the decade-long rivalry between Ironblades quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]] and [[Okami Direwolves]] defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]], whose annual matchup is the most-watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken.

==History==
===Founding and early years (1985–1992)===
The X-League was formally established on March 15, 1985, by the [[Sanese Lurandian Football Association]] (SLFA) as a consolidation of the existing corporate amateur Lurandian Football circuit. The founding six teams were all backed by major Sanese industrial conglomerates, reflecting the corporate sports model that also defined Okaiken's rugby and basketball leagues.

The inaugural 1985 season featured:
* [[Saitama Heavy Industries|Saitama Forges]] (now Saitama Samurai)
* [[Koshien Steel Corporation|Koshien Steelworkers]] (now Koshien Steelers)
* [[Toyozawa Electric|Toyozawa Ironblades]]
* [[Okami Financial Group|Okami Direwolves]]
* Aizawa Shipbuilding (now Aizawa Iron Hawks)
* Yokohama Marine (now Yokohama Steel)

The first Rice Bowl under the X-League banner was held on January 12, 1986, with the Toyozawa Ironblades defeating the Saitama Forges 24–17 in front of 32,000 fans at Toyozawa Municipal Stadium. The Ironblades, led by star fullback [[Shigeo Arakawa]], established early dominance, winning four of the first six Rice Bowl titles.

===Expansion era (1993–2010)===
The league expanded to eight teams in 1993 with the addition of the [[Fukuoka Kraken]] and the [[Sendai Blitz]], reflecting the sport's geographic spread beyond the Kanto-Kansai corridor. The expansion coincided with the first television broadcasting agreement, which brought X-League games to a national audience via the OR Sports network.

The 1990s were defined by the rise of the [[Okami Direwolves]], who won three consecutive Rice Bowl titles from 1997 to 1999 behind the ferocious pass rush of defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo Shibata]]. Shibata's arrival in 1995 transformed the Direwolves from a middling franchise into a defensive powerhouse, and his individual rivalry with Toyozawa quarterbacks became the league's defining narrative.

The league expanded to ten teams in 2001, adding the [[Kanto Thunder]] and the [[Nagoya Vipers]]. The 2000s saw increasingly competitive balance, with five different franchises winning the Rice Bowl between 2000 and 2010.

===Modernization and Forge State era (2011–present)===
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 and the ascendancy of the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology brought unprecedented attention and investment to the X-League. The sport's emphasis on armored physical confrontation aligned naturally with the national philosophy, and government investment through the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] designated Lurandian Football as a priority sport for youth development.

The league underwent a comprehensive modernization in 2014:
* Introduction of a two-division structure (Division 1 and Division 2)
* Implementation of a salary cap and rookie draft system (2016)
* Expansion to twelve teams with the addition of the [[Mito Blacksmiths]] and the [[Sapporo Frost]] (2019)
* New broadcasting agreements with DAZN Okaiken and Samurai TV, increasing annual revenue by 340%

The modern X-League era has been defined by the [[Ryuichi Nakada]]–[[Kengo Shibata]] rivalry. Nakada, drafted first overall by the Ironblades in 2016, brought a punishing rushing style and surgical passing accuracy that revitalized the Toyozawa franchise. His annual clashes with Shibata's Direwolves have become the league's marquee event, with the 2024 regular season matchup drawing a record 52.3 million viewers—the highest-rated regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history for any sport.

