==Notable players and rivalries==
===Ryuichi Nakada vs. Kengo "The Wall" Shibata===
The defining rivalry of the modern X-League is the decade-long clash between [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] quarterback '''[[Ryuichi Nakada]]''' and [[Okami Direwolves]] defensive end '''[[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]]'''. Nakada, drafted first overall in 2016, is renowned for his punishing rushing style—he leads all X-League quarterbacks in career rushing yards by a wide margin—and his strategic brilliance in reading defensive formations. Shibata, a three-time Defensive Player of the Year, is the most feared pass rusher in league history, holding the all-time X-League sack record.

Their annual matchup is the most-watched regular season broadcast in Okaiken, with the 2024 meeting drawing 52.3 million viewers. The rivalry has become a cultural touchstone, frequently cited in popular media as a metaphor for the Sanese ideals of strategic attack and unyielding defense.

===Other notable players===
* '''[[Shigeo Arakawa]]''' — Fullback, Toyozawa Ironblades (1985–1998). The first superstar of the X-League, Arakawa's punishing rushing style earned him the nickname "The Freight Train." He led the Ironblades to four of the first six Rice Bowl titles and remains the league's all-time leading rusher.
* '''[[Tatsuo Kanzaki]]''' — Quarterback, Okami Direwolves (1993–2005). A precision passer who revolutionized the X-League passing game, Kanzaki led the Direwolves to their first Rice Bowl title in 1995 and mentored a young Kengo Shibata early in his career.
* '''[[Jun Morimoto]]''' — Wide receiver, Saitama Samurai (2001–2014). The most prolific receiver in X-League history, Morimoto holds the all-time records for receptions, receiving yards, and receiving touchdowns. His acrobatic catches and fierce competitive drive made him a fan favorite across the league.
* '''[[Daigo Kuga]]''' — Linebacker, Fukuoka Kraken (2010–2023). A ferocious defensive captain who led the Kraken to two Rice Bowl titles, Kuga was known for his sideline-to-sideline speed and devastating open-field tackles. His final game—the 2023 Rice Bowl—was a 17-point defensive masterpiece against the Aizawa Iron Hawks.

